NAME
       stat - show the details of a file: size, owner, permissions, times

SYNOPSIS
       stat [OPTION]... FILE...

DESCRIPTION
       stat prints everything the file system keeps about a file (its inode):
       size, number of blocks, the kind of file, the device it lives on, the
       inode number, the number of hard links, the permissions in octal and
       in letters, the owner and group (names and numbers), the SELinux
       context, and four times:

       Access   when the contents were last read,
       Modify   when the contents were last changed,
       Change   when the inode was last changed (owner, permissions, links,
                and every contents change too),
       Birth    when the file was created, where the file system records it.

       With -c, stat prints only the parts you name, which makes it the best
       way to check one property in a script or a test.

OPTIONS
       -c, --format=FORMAT
              Print FORMAT for each file, followed by a newline. Common
              sequences:
                  %n  file name               %s  size in bytes
                  %a  permissions in octal    %A  permissions in letters
                  %U  owner name              %u  owner UID
                  %G  group name              %g  group GID
                  %F  kind of file            %h  number of hard links
                  %i  inode number            %C  SELinux context
                  %x  last access             %y  last modification
                  %z  last status change      %Y  modification, in seconds
                                                  since 1970

       --printf=FORMAT
              Like -c, but backslash escapes such as \n are understood and no
              newline is added.

       -L, --dereference
              Follow a symbolic link and show the file it points to.

       -f, --file-system
              Show the file system the file is on (type, block size, free
              blocks, free inodes) instead of the file.

       -t, --terse
              Print all the details on one line.

EXAMPLES
       Show everything about /etc/passwd:

           stat /etc/passwd

       Check the octal permissions and owner of a shared directory:

           stat -c '%a %U:%G' /srv/shared

       Print the SELinux context of a web page:

           stat -c %C /var/www/html/index.html

       Check that two names are hard links to the same file (same inode):

           stat -c '%i %h %n' /root/a /root/b

NOTES
       In octal permissions from %a, a leading 2 means set-group-ID (2770),
       a 1 the sticky bit (1777) and a 4 set-user-ID.

SEE ALSO
       ls(1), chmod(1), touch(1), df(1)
